        {
            "id": "transactions_daily",
            "display_name": "E-commerce Transactions Daily (by transaction)",
            "_comment": "Epic 17 story 17.4: GA4 e-commerce transactions = conversions + purchaseRevenue by transactionId at the day grain. This is the GA4 side of the Shopify x GA4 reconciliation (doc/synthese §3.2): the transaction_reconciliation mart FULL OUTER JOINs stg_ga4_transactions_daily against stg_shopify_orders_daily by (project_id, date, transaction_id) and MEASURES the coverage rate. GRAIN DISCIPLINE (RULE ABSOLUE, lesson epic-10 + decision 15.4): the transaction grain is a JOIN KEY, NOT a breakdown partition -- per-transaction rows do NOT total the connector day, so this profile has NO fact_daily_kpi UNION block (contrast every other GA4 profile). The raw table feeds ONLY stg_ga4_transactions_daily -> transaction_reconciliation. AI-53 honesty: transactionId (GA4 Data API v1 e-commerce dimension) and conversions / purchaseRevenue metric names are DECLARED per the doc but NOT verified live (no property connected; e-commerce tracking is a property-level setup). AC / AI-13 confirms exact names via properties/{p}/metadata (Phase B gate). A lead-gen site with no e-commerce returns ZERO transaction rows -> reconciliation degrades to 'no GA4 transactions' honestly. dimensions lead with 'date' (manifest contract). PAGINATION (point dur): a day can carry more transactions than one page; unlike the acquisition/pages top-N leaderboards, transactions are a JOIN KEY -- dropping the tail would understate the coverage denominator, so the pull ORDER BY transactionId + pages on offset to completeness within a documented bound (row_limit = per-request page size, env GA4_TXN_LIMIT; _MAX_TXN_PAGES caps the offset loop; any truncation is logged, never silent). verification_expected_rows_per_day = null (transaction cardinality is unbounded per day; it is not a fixed-N leaderboard).",
            "metrics": [
                "conversions",
                "purchase_revenue"
            ],
            "dimensions": [
                "date",
                "transaction_id"
            ],
            "extraction_capabilities": {
                "row_limit": 10000,
                "row_limit_min": 50,
                "row_limit_max": 100000,
                "filters_supported": true,
                "realtime": false
            },
            "extraction_path": "custom_pull",
            "verification_expected_rows_per_day": null
        },
        {
            "id": "catalog_daily",
            "display_name": "Catalog-driven Daily Report",
            "_comment": "Story 25.9 (extends the 25.8 meta-ads reference): the catalog IS the execution contract. Any cataloged GA4 field a datastream selects is extractable at execution -- core validates the selection against api_catalog.json and passes it into pull_catalog_daily, which builds the runReport dimensions[]+metrics[] from the selection. selection_mode=catalog_driven; the metrics/dimensions listed here are the tier-core default carriers (what a None selection resolves to, pruned of realtime-incompatible minute dims). GA4 hard limits: a single runReport takes at most 10 metrics AND 9 dimensions; a wider selection CHUNKS into >1 runReport (date carried in every chunk; dimension-chunks cross metric-chunks) and the per-chunk rows MERGE on the (date, breakdown) grain key. Realtime-only minute dims (dateHourMinute/minute/nthMinute) are refused at request-build with invalid_request (catalog_sources.realtime_incompatible_fields). Lands in the dedicated long raw_ga4_catalog_daily (date, metric_name, metric_value, breakdown_dimension, breakdown_value). The legacy exact_bundle profiles + raw_ga4_standard_daily are UNTOUCHED (AD-22).",
            "metrics": [
                "sessions",
                "active_users",
                "conversions",
                "screen_page_views",
                "purchase_revenue"
            ],
