    "_error_map_note": "Story 25.7 (playbook step 4): google-analytics declares NO error_map. Rationale -- core.pull_errors._extract_provider_code reads error.code from the JSON body, and the GA4 Data API (like all Google APIs) sets error.code to the NUMERIC HTTP status (e.g. 401 for UNAUTHENTICATED, 403 for PERMISSION_DENIED). So any refinement key would be either redundant with the pure-HTTP classification (401:401->auth_expired already holds via _base_class_for_status) or unreachable: the actually-discriminating tokens Google returns (error.status string 'PERMISSION_DENIED' / 'RESOURCE_EXHAUSTED', or error.errors[].reason 'rateLimitExceeded' / 'userRateLimitExceeded') are NOT what the generic extractor keys on, and core must never hardcode a provider vocabulary (AD-2 / HG-1). The pure-HTTP mapping is correct for GA4: 401->auth_expired (reconnect), 403->permission_denied, 400->invalid_request (catalog drift), 5xx->provider_transient (retry), 429->RateLimitError (breaker, handled before classify_http_error). The connector's non-429 raise sites therefore call classify_http_error(status, body) with no map and are LEFT UNCHANGED. If GA4 ever needs a 403 quota-vs-permission split, the correct fix is a richer core extractor (read error.status), not a per-provider code map.",
